Privacy Audit of Popular Apps (Desktop WhatsApp) and Websites (Facebook) & Data Breach Case Study Analysis

PART 1: Setup WhatsApp Desktop
Step 1: Install Node.js and npm
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nodejs npm -y
Step 2: Install Nativefier
sudo npm install -g nativefier
Step 3: Create WhatsApp Desktop App
nativefier https://web.whatsapp.com
👉 A folder will be created like:
web-whatsapp-linux-x64
Step 4: Run WhatsApp Desktop
cd WhatsAppWeb-linux-x64
./WhatsAppWeb
Step 5: Login
Open mobile WhatsApp
Go to Linked Devices
Scan QR code

PART 2: Privacy Audit of WhatsApp
Step 6: Analyze Trackers (Exodus Privacy)
Open browser
Go to:
https://reports.exodus-privacy.eu.org
Search for WhatsApp
Observation
Trackers detected:
Google Analytics
Crashlytics
Facebook Analytics
👉 Record:
Number of trackers
Permissions used
Step 7: Start Wireshark
wireshark
Select interface (eth0 / wlan0)
Click Start Capture
Step 8: Generate Network Traffic
In WhatsApp Desktop:
Send messages
Send images
Open chats
Step 9: Apply Filters in Wireshark
Use:
tls
dns
Step 10: Analyze Traffic
Observations
Traffic is encrypted (TLS)
Domains observed:
whatsapp.net
facebook.com
Findings
End-to-End Encryption present
Message content NOT visible
Metadata visible:
IP address
Time
Server communication

PART 3: Privacy Audit of Facebook Website
Step 11: Open Facebook
Go to:
👉 Facebook
Login using a test account
Step 12: Start Burp Suite
burpsuite
Step 13: Configure Proxy
In Burp:
IP: 127.0.0.1
Port: 8080
In Firefox:
Settings → Network → Manual Proxy
Enter same IP and Port
Step 14: Intercept Traffic
Turn Intercept ON
Reload Facebook
Observe
Cookies:
datr
fr
Session IDs
HTTP headers
Step 15: Analyze Tracking (Browser DevTools)
Press F12 → Network tab
Look for:
Ads
Analytics scripts
Third-party requests
Step 16: Findings (Facebook)
Heavy tracking observed
Uses cookies for personalization
Third-party integrations present

